    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-348691",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e520dc67-3b63-5ca7-959b-f642d85529d6",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Magnitude of a Vector-Valued Function",
      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t+3},\\,3\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩,$ what is $|r(1)|?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$|r(1)|=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Evaluating the function at $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\sqrt{1+3},\\,3\\,{e}^{1-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{4},\\,3\\,{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(1)| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
